   "id": "ap1-1.1-005",
   "obj": "1.1",
   "diff": 2,
   "q": "After replacing a laptop's M.2 Wi-Fi card, a technician finds the adapter is detected and connects, but the signal is very weak even next to the access point. What should be checked first?",
   "choices": [
    "Antenna leads are snapped onto the card",
    "The newest Wi-Fi driver is installed",
    "The Wi-Fi radio is enabled in UEFI setup",
    "Airplane mode is turned off in Windows"
   ],
   "answer": 0,
   "why": "Wi-Fi antennas usually run through the display lid and attach to the card with small snap-on leads. A missing or loose lead after a card swap leaves the card working but with very weak signal, so check the leads first.",
   "whyNot": {
    "1": "A driver problem usually stops the adapter from working; it does not explain a weak signal beside the access point right after a card swap.",
    "2": "A radio disabled in UEFI would hide the adapter entirely, but this one is detected and connects.",
    "3": "Airplane mode turns the radio off, but this adapter is connected, just with weak signal."
   },
